nd library guidance that all adoption code builds\nagainst — the strategy itself states adoptions are independent \"once the convention\nexists\"); E002..E009→E010 are Rule 11 docs-authoring edges (valid, and exempt from\npriority inheritance); no edges between the adoption epics (Rule 14 — different repos,\nno shared artifacts), so naturally parallel work is not serialized. No\nkonflux-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints do not apply.\nNo configuration-reference-only edges (no image digest/SHA/endpoint justifications).\nNo branch files, so no `branch`/`gated_by` branch-file checks apply. Critical path\nlength 3 (E001 → adoption → E010) is the minimum structurally possible given the\nconvention prerequisite and Rule 11 fan-in; no missing data/artifact edges were found\nin epic content (E010's consumption of each component's sample log output is captured\nby its dependencies on E002–E009).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEach component/team tuple produces a separate epic. Component/team assignments were\nverified against `.context/architecture-context/` owning-team fields: Dashboard→RHOAI\nDashboard, KServe→RHOAI Serving, Data Science Pipelines→RHOAI Pipelines, Model\nRegistry→Model Registry, TrustyAI→TrustyAI, Notebooks→Workbenches, Training\nOperator→Distributed Workloads — all correct. E003 (KServe) and E004 (Serving\nRuntimes) are correctly split as distinct components despite the same team. E005\nbundles the DSP operator and API server (same component, same team, same logical\nchange) and E007 bundles the TrustyAI operator and services — both permitted by the\nsame-logical-change rule. No scope duplication across siblings: each epic converts\nonly its own component's logging; no two epics build the same surface. No work-product\ntype mixing: E010 (docs-authoring) is cleanly separated from code implementation, and\nE001 produces a specification artifact without bundling code. No epic exceeds ~2 weeks\n(the adoptions are mechanical format conversions; E004 spans three runtime images but\nis one logical conformance change within one component). One nit: E001's team \"RHOAI\nPlatform\" is a placeholder not traceable to architecture context — flagged by the\ndecomposer in Health Warnings and recorded as a minor issue below.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nNo Investigation epics — correct, since the strategy's Open Questions section is\nexplicitly \"None\" and HLR-1 is prerequisite design producing a published artifact (an\nImplementation), not uncertainty resolution. Every Implementation epic produces a\nconcrete artifact (published convention, code changes, published documentation). No\n`gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, which is correct: there are\nno gates (no external dependencies with uncertain timing — the only gating artifact is\nproduced inside this strategy, as the Tiered Delivery section notes). E010's\n`implementation_type: docs-authoring` is correct for HLR-9.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nAll ten epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) in\nfrontmatter with `investigation_signals: null` — type-appropriate. Every epic has an\nai-signals rationale file, and frontmatter values match the rationale tables exactly.\nSignal values are consistent with strategy content: E001 justifiably diverges from the\nadoption epics (change_specificity 1 because HLR-1 enumerates the deliverable;\nexisting_foundation 0 for greenfield standard; human_process_gates -1 for cross-team\nacknowledgement). E004's divergence is well-reasoned and honest: external_dependency\n-1 (upstream-vendored runtimes), architecture_claims 0 (no dedicated architecture\ncontext file for Serving Runtimes), and open_questions 0 reflecting the per-runtime\nupstream-vs-midstream decision that changes how (not whether) the work happens — the\nrationale matches the epic's scope and risk notes. E005's external_dependency 0\n(upstream-derived API server) is consistent with its architecture context. Per\ninstructions, computed `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` values were\nnot checked.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text: schema validation of captured\nsample logs (pass/fail), correlation-id/namespace presence in log records, build\npipeline green, and consumer-tolerance-or-documented-fallback. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent where applicable: E010 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-\nimplemented-output AC; every adoption epic carries a build-pipeline-green AC; the\nconsumer-fallback ACs serve the mitigation role for the log-format replacements. No\nnon-testable schedule-constraint ACs were found (no lab-turnaround or review-cadence\nACs misfiled). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (grep over all epic\nbodies found none; E003's traceability note says \"tracked separately by scope\" rather\nthan naming E004).\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is covered: every HLR, the cross-component correlation goal from\nthe problem statement (correlation-id ACs in each adoption epic plus the\ncross-component query examples in E010), and the affected-components list. Out-of-scope\nboundaries (log storage/forwarding, metrics/tracing) are respected — no epic touches\nthem. Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: the missing strategy\nsections (Impacted Teams, ACs, NFRs) are flagged in Health Warnings with the inference\nprocedure documented; the serving-runtimes scope ambiguity and the upstream coupling\nrisk are captured (the latter in E004's Risks/Notes, correctly not as a DAG edge).\nNo conditional branches are needed (no bounded outcomes in the strategy). The\ndocs-authoring epic (E010) exists and covers HLR-9. Minor note: E004's component\n\"Serving Runtimes\" is absent from the strategy's Affected Components list and has no\ndedicated architecture context file — the decomposition flags this ambig