gnals: null` — type-appropriate. Every epic has an\n98\tai-signals rationale file, and frontmatter values match the rationale tables exactly.\n99\tSignal values are consistent with strategy content: E001 justifiably diverges from the\n100\tadoption epics (change_specificity 1 because HLR-1 enumerates the deliverable;\n101\texisting_foundation 0 for greenfield standard; human_process_gates -1 for cross-team\n102\tacknowledgement). E004's divergence is well-reasoned and honest: external_dependency\n103\t-1 (upstream-vendored runtimes), architecture_claims 0 (no dedicated architecture\n104\tcontext file for Serving Runtimes), and open_questions 0 reflecting the per-runtime\n105\tupstream-vs-midstream decision that changes how (not whether) the work happens — the\n106\trationale matches the epic's scope and risk notes. E005's external_dependency 0\n107\t(upstream-derived API server) is consistent with its architecture context. Per\n108\tinstructions, computed `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` values were\n109\tnot checked.\n110\t\n111\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n112\t\n113\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text: schema validation of captured\n114\tsample logs (pass/fail), correlation-id/namespace presence in log records, build\n115\tpipeline green, and consumer-tolerance-or-documented-fallback. Rule-mandated ACs are\n116\tpresent where applicable: E010 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-\n117\timplemented-output AC; every adoption epic carries a build-pipeline-green AC; the\n118\tconsumer-fallback ACs serve the mitigation role for the log-format replacements. No\n119\tnon-testable schedule-constraint ACs were found (no lab-turnaround or review-cadence\n120\tACs misfiled). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (grep over all epic\n121\tbodies found none; E003's traceability note says \"tracked separately by scope\" rather\n122\tthan naming E004).\n123\t\n124\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n125\t\n126\t